{
  "plancraftGrid": 1,
  "name": "Retirement decision grid v4",
  "description": "FERS: 7/8 VA appointment, service from 2023, high-3 = FTE base+market $275k per 38 USC 7431(f). Social Security claimed at 70 for both; Simone's later US career start (~2022) gives $4,100/mo real if she works to 60, $2,700/mo if she stops in 2038. Michael's SS $3,900-$5,100/mo by retirement age. Disability: income stops 2033, DI nets $25k/yr savings then $180k/yr to 65, FERS $1,278/mo from 62, SS $2,900/mo from 70. Base: house June 2027, 2 kids, savings +2.5%/yr real, PA. COLLEGE (v3): out-of-pocket beyond the 529 ($1,300/mo/child birth-18), at $0/$25k/$50k per year per child over each child's college years (2045-49 and 2047-51), as essential expenses. HEALTH COVERAGE (v4): Michael's MRA is 57, so separating at 50 (16 yrs service) or 55 (21 yrs) allows only a DEFERRED annuity, which permanently forfeits FEHB; retiring at 60 (26 yrs) or 65 earns an immediate unreduced annuity and keeps it. The four early-retirement patterns carry an unsubsidized-coverage bridge from the year neither spouse has employer coverage until Michael turns 65 in 2054: M50/spouse-stops-2038 runs 2039-2054 (15 yrs, $28k/yr), M55/spouse-stops-2038 2044-2054 (10 yrs, $33k/yr), both spouse-to-60 patterns 2048-2054 (6 yrs, $21k/yr). Amounts are INCREMENTAL over what FEHB would have cost (~$10.5k/yr all-in), isolating the FEHB-loss penalty rather than double-counting coverage the other branches also buy. Built from the Philadelphia 2026 gold index rate (~$315/mo at age 21) x federal age factors at each window's midpoint, plus out-of-pocket, uplifted ~1.5%/yr for real medical inflation. Unsubsidized is correct: enhanced premium tax credits expired 31 Dec 2025 and the 400% FPL cliff is back ($84,600 MAGI for a couple). Unmodeled levers: managing MAGI under the cliff, and a VA early-out (VERA), which would grant an immediate annuity at M55 and preserve FEHB. SS amounts are scheduled benefits; the trust-fund shortfall is not modeled.",
